// =============================================================================
// MemQL Trait Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `trait` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it).
//
// A trait is the one deliberately UNBOUND boolean predicate (epic #2281;
// docs/internal/design/spec-shape-binding-adr.md). Same runtime contract as
// a spec, but a trait carries NO signature binding -- it tests a property
// that can apply to rows from many different concepts (e.g. "active record",
// "cancelled record", "running"). It is the principled exception to spec
// signature binding: unbound BECAUSE it is generic.
//
// Rules:
//   - Signature `trait <name>` -- NO bound name.
//   - Body `return <boolean expression>` over BARE payload field names (no
//     `payload.` prefix). Classifies as a row-spec (compiles to a SQL WHERE
//     fragment, pushed down to the database).
//   - A trait may NOT reference actor.* or row.* -- it binds no @actor /
//     @row gateway shape. A caller predicate is a `spec` bound to an @actor
//     shape; a row-metadata predicate is a `spec` bound to a @row shape.
//   - Bare fields are validated against the CONCRETE concept at the call
//     site (a query filter, or composition into a bound spec).
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Trait-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
//   @description("text")   Human-readable description.
//   @enabled               Accepted no-op: traits are enabled by default.
//   @disabled              Parsed but never registered (#2607).
//
// @shape / @row / @actor / @useConcept(...) and the rest of the retired
// @use* family are all rejected on a trait (a trait binds nothing).
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Row predicate traits -- the common case
// =============================================================================
//
// Bare payload field name, `return` body, comparison against a literal.

@description("Matches records with active==true field.")
trait isActiveRecord {
  return active == true
}

@description("Matches records with status=='cancelled'.")
trait isCancelled {
  return status == "cancelled"
}

@description("Matches records that are not soft-deleted (deleted!=true).")
trait isNotDeleted {
  return deleted != true
}

@description("Matches records whose status sits in the live set.")
trait isLive {
  return status == "active" || status == "saved"
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Row intrinsics -- bare-name references inside traits
// =============================================================================
//
// A trait body may also reference intrinsic row fields by their bare name:
//
//     id          -- row id
//     createdAt   -- insert timestamp
//     createdBy   -- author identity id
//     concept     -- canonical concept id (v1:ns:name)
//     type        -- the concept's @type value
//     schema      -- schema variant id
//     partition   -- partition the row lives in
//
// These are engine-stamped intrinsics, not payload fields; the resolver
// keeps them bare (a non-intrinsic bare name resolves to payload.<name>).

@description("Matches rows from the cognition:participant concept.")
trait isParticipantRow {
  return concept == "v1:cognition:participant"
}

@description("Matches rows authored by a specific known identity.")
trait isSeedRow {
  return createdBy == "seed"
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Caller predicates are SPECS, not traits
// =============================================================================
//
// A trait may NOT read actor.* -- it binds no @actor gateway. Author a
// caller predicate as a `spec` bound to an @actor shape (see _spec.memql):
//
//     use common.shapes.{ actorEnvelope }
//
//     @description("Caller must hold an admin or owner role.")
//     spec actorEnvelope requiresAdmin {
//       return role == "admin" || role == "owner"
//     }
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. Annotations / forms REJECTED on traits
// =============================================================================
//
//     // REJECTED: a trait binds nothing -- no signature binding.
//     trait someShape traitWithBinding { return active == true }
//
//     // REJECTED: @shape is removed; @row/@actor are shape-only.
//     @shape("participantFull")
//     trait traitOfShape { return active == true }
//
//     // REJECTED: a trait may not read actor.* (no gateway). Use a spec.
//     trait traitReadsActor { return role == "admin" }
//
//     // REJECTED: the bare-expression body (no `return`) is retired.
//     trait isActive { active == true }
//
//     // REJECTED: the receiver-function form never existed.
//     func (Trait) isActive(_ any) bool { return payload.active == true }
//
// =============================================================================
//   6. Calling traits
// =============================================================================
//
//   - From a query's filter clause: list the trait name alongside other
//     predicates, joined with `&&`:
//
//         query foo {
//           filter payload.partitionId==args.partitionId && isActiveRecord
//           shape  fooFull
//         }
//
//   - Composed into a concept- or shape-bound spec at the call site.
//
// =============================================================================
//   7. Lifecycle: @enabled and @disabled
// =============================================================================
//
//   - absent or @enabled = registered (enabled is the default; the
//     annotation is an accepted no-op).
//   - @disabled = parse the trait but skip registration (#2607).

@description("Live, registered.")
trait liveExample {
  return active == true
}

@disabled
@description("Parsed but skipped at registration. Remove @disabled to re-enable.")
trait parkedExample {
  return active == true
}
